artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider hole-doped perovskite manganites of the general form R_{1-x}A_x MnO_3, where R is a trivalent rare-earth cation (e.g., La, Pr, Nd) and A is a divalent alkaline-earth cation (e.g., Ca, Sr, Ba), and x denotes the hole concentration. Such compounds exhibit colossal magnetoresistance and, depending on x and temperature, ground states ranging from antiferromagnetic charge/orbital-ordered insulators to ferromagnetic metals, with widespread theoretical and experimental indications of intrinsic electronic inhomogeneity ("phase separation") into coexisting nanoscale domains of competing phases.

Carry out atomic-resolution scanning tunneling microscopy (STM) and scanning tunneling spectroscopy (STS) measurements on cleaved single-crystal or epitaxial-film surfaces of such manganites across a broad range of hole densities x, with particular emphasis on compositions whose low-temperature ground state is a ferromagnetic metal. Determine, as functions of x, temperature, and applied magnetic field:

(a) the existence, spatial morphology, length scales, and volume fractions of coexisting electronic phases (e.g., metallic versus insulating, charge-ordered versus uniform) at the atomic and nanometer scales;
(b) the local density of states and local single-particle gap (or pseudogap) within each phase, including the spatial profile across phase boundaries;
(c) the correlation of these local electronic features with the underlying lattice and any observed charge/orbital ordering patterns;
(d) how the inhomogeneous electronic landscape evolves through the ferromagnetic-metal/insulator transition and across the CMR regime.

Provide a coherent description of phase separation and local electronic structure in manganites obtained from these atomic-resolution STM/STS data over the full hole-doping range, including the ferromagnetic-metallic compositions.

